Git and GitHub are not merged into one thing. They work together.

Think of it like this:

Your Computer                    Internet
─────────────                    ────────


Git                               GitHub
Tracks code history      →       Stores code online

======

How to connect Git with GitHub
Step 1: Create a project
my-project/
├── app.py
├── requirements.txt
└── README.md

=====

Step 2: Initialize Git

Open the terminal inside your project:

git init

Now Git starts tracking your project.


==========

Step 3: Add files
git add .

This tells Git:

"I want to include all these files."

========

Step 4: Commit
git commit -m "Initial project"

This creates a saved version in Git.

======

Step 5: Create a repository on GitHub

On GitHub, create a new repository, for example:

my-ai-project
Step 6: Connect local Git to GitHub

GitHub gives you a repository URL. Connect it:

git remote add origin YOUR_GITHUB_REPOSITORY_URL

Then push your code:

git branch -M main
git push -u origin main

======

Full connection flow
YOUR COMPUTER
┌──────────────────────┐
│                      │
│  Write Code          │
│       ↓              │
│  git add .           │
│       ↓              │
│  git commit          │
│       ↓              │
│       GIT            │
└──────────┬───────────┘
           │
           │ git push
           ↓
┌──────────────────────┐
│       GITHUB         │
│                      │
│ Your project online  │
└──────────────────────┘

======

After making future changes
git add .
git commit -m "Updated chatbot feature"
git push

So the simple relationship is:

Git = saves and tracks your code versions.
GitHub = stores your Git project online.
git push = sends your Git changes to GitHub.

And this is where CI/CD can start:

Git → GitHub → GitHub Actions → Test/Build → Deploy 🚀

